## Claim
Text: "Joe Biden said he wants to ban fracking in the US."
Claim author: Charlie Kirk of the conservative student organization Turning Point USA
Claim date: October 23, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210506043822/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hV8_Bunawqg

## Initial Q&A
### Did Joe Biden say, in his own words, that he wants to ban fracking in the United States?
Answer: Based on the search result, Joe Biden did not say he wants to ban fracking; instead, he explicitly stated, "Fracking is not going to be on the chopping block," and clarified that he would not shut down the industry but would only restrict new leases on federal land.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793

### On what date did Joe Biden allegedly make the statement about banning fracking?
Answer: According to the search result, Joe Biden made the statement about eliminating fossil fuels, including fracking, during a CNN Democratic presidential debate on July 31, 2019, when he said, “we would make sure it’s eliminated” in response to a question about the place for fossil fuels and fracking in a Biden administration.

Source URL: https://www.factcheck.org/2020/06/misleading-ad-targets-biden-on-fossil-fuels-fracking/

### In what specific event or forum (e.g., a debate, interview, campaign rally) did Joe Biden make the statement about banning fracking?
Answer: Based on the search result, Joe Biden made a statement about banning fracking during a 2019 debate, where he said his administration would make sure fracking is "eliminated," and also during a March Democratic debate, where he said "no more, no new fracking." However, the search result does not specify the exact name or date of a single event, so the most direct reference is the 2019 debate.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-does-joe-biden-support-green-new-deal-fracking-ban-1537291

### Did Joe Biden's statement refer to banning fracking on all land in the United States, or only on federal lands?
Answer: Joe Biden's statement clarified that he does not support a nationwide ban on fracking, but he has expressed support for stopping "all oil drilling or gas drilling on federal lands." Thus, his proposed restriction applies only to federal lands, not all land in the United States.

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

### Is there a verifiable audio or video recording of Joe Biden making the statement about banning fracking?
Answer: No, the search result does not contain a verifiable audio or video recording of Joe Biden making a statement about banning fracking. Instead, it directly quotes him saying, “I am not banning fracking,” and explains that his campaign has clarified he only supports no new fracking permits on federal lands.

Source URL: https://grist.org/politics/why-wont-biden-ban-fracking-speech-pennsylvania/

### Did Joe Biden use the exact words "ban fracking" in his statement, or did he use comparable language such as "end fracking" or "transition away from fracking"?
Answer: Based on the search result, Joe Biden did not use the words "ban fracking" or comparable language such as "end fracking" or "transition away from fracking"; instead, he explicitly stated, "I am not banning fracking. Let me say that again: I am not banning fracking."

Source URL: https://www.uagetinvolved.org/content/joe-biden-i-am-not-banning-fracking

### Did Joe Biden make any subsequent clarifying or contradictory statements about banning fracking after the date of the original claim?
Answer: Yes, after the claim date of October 23, 2020, Joe Biden made clarifying statements that contradicted the idea that he wanted to ban fracking entirely. For instance, he told a Pennsylvania ABC News affiliate, "Well, fracking is not going to be on the chopping block," and had previously stated in an April 2020 interview that he would not shut down the fracking industry but would merely halt new fracking leases on federal land.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793

### What was Joe Biden's official policy position on fracking as publicly documented by his 2020 presidential campaign at the time of the claim date?
Answer: According to the Newsweek article, Joe Biden's official campaign position at the time of the claim date was that fracking would not be banned; he explicitly stated, "Fracking is not going to be on the chopping block." The article further clarifies that Biden "said he wouldn't shut down the fracking industry but merely wouldn't allow new fracking leases on federal land, which only represents 10 percent of all national leases," and his campaign website described the Green New Deal as "a crucial framework for meeting the climate challenges we face."

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/biden-says-fracking-jobs-wont-chopping-block-if-hes-elected-1516793

## Final Judgement
The fact-check evidence consistently shows that Joe Biden explicitly stated he does not want to ban fracking. He said, "I am not banning fracking" and "fracking is not going to be on the chopping block." His policy was to halt new fracking leases on federal land only, which is not a ban on fracking overall. While he made a 2019 debate comment about eliminating fossil fuels, subsequent clarifications and his official campaign position contradicted any intent to ban fracking. Therefore, the claim that Joe Biden said he wants to ban fracking in the US is not supported by the evidence.

